{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi","id":1082292,"title":"An alternate Olympus/Dodekatheon/Theoi","path":["Home","Onyx Path","Onyx Path Worlds","Scion"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-04-22T09:40:57-07:00","id":1082292,"title":"An alternate Olympus/Dodekatheon/Theoi","content":"So, in Greek Mythology, Gaia, a little annoyed at her son Kronos for castrating his dad Ouranus, told him that one of his kids would supplant him the same way. So he ate them all, until Zeus got swapped out for a rock and grew up to fulfill the prophecy. Flash forward and, again annoyed for reasons, Gaia tell Zeus that the kid he's about to have with Metis will supplant him. So he decides to outdo his own dad and just eat Metis before she even gives birth. And a bit later, a fully formed and grown up Athena pops out of Zeus's skull. <br /> <br /> My thought is that, sometime in, say the middle ages, Athena finally fulfills her destiny and takes over the Greek pantheon. Zeus, Hera, Hades, and maybe Demeter and Poseidon are imprisoned in Tartarus. Ares, a loyalist and never liked by Athena, is banished from Olympus and forced to walk the earth. Aphrodite, also having a mutual hate-on with the new queen, also leaves. <br /> <br /> So, the current pantheon (a Dekatheon, if you will) looks like:<ul><li><b>Athena</b>, Tyrant of Olympus, Goddess of Wisdom, Craft, and War, Ruler of the Heavens and wielder of both the divine thunderbolt and the aegis. She considers the realms of Science and Technology to fall under her purview. A rather cold-blooded and super-logical dictator who is always right and willing to commit numerous brutalities in the name of the &quot;greater good&quot; of order and progress. The sort of leader who'd fit in perfectly well in 1984, Brave New World, or some similar dystopia.</li> <li><b>Apollo</b>, the Sun King, God of Truth, Prophecy, Music, Art, Medicine and Light, the platonic consort of Athena. Like Athena, he's also a patron of modern science. Very much the ideal of ordered creation in rejection of unrestrained chaos (drawing heavily on the theories of Camilla Pagilia and Fredrick Nietzsche). Attended by the Nine Muses.</li> <li><b>Persephone</b>, Goddess of the Earth, producer and destroyer of all living things, keeper of the Underworld and jailer of Tartarus, having originally transcended the role of daughter by becoming the willing wife of Hades, she has now transcended that role by becoming a dark Queen in her own right.</li> <li><b>Hermes</b>, God of Transitions and Boundaries, Messenger of Olympus, patron and overseer of business (including organized crime, modern professional sports, politics and mass media) travel and the Internet. A rogue and one of the few willing to speak truth to the power of Athena.</li> <li><b>Artemis</b>, Goddess of the Wild, the Hunt, Childbirth and of Youth, she now also serves as the Assassin of Olympus, charged with hunting and slaying enemies of Athena. Her loyalty to the regime is primarily a personal one to her brother.</li> <li><b>Hephaestus</b>, God of the Forge, Metal, Fire and Invention, the chief engineer and armorer of the gods, also holding modern technology as part of her purview. As always, he remains hopelessly in love with Athena, who is unwilling (if not unable) to reciprocate.</li> <li><b>Hestia</b>, Goddess of Hearth and Home, and of the State. The only remaining member of the six original gods, both because she is loyal to Olympus itself and not individual rulers, and because as the oldest child of Kronos and Rhea and essentially the goddess of the foundation of human civilization, it is feared that her personal power may be too great to challenge.</li> <li><b>Dionysus</b>, God of Ecstasy, Fertility, Tragedy and Comedy, the Devil's Advocate of an Olympus devoted to order. Almost the antithesis of Apollo, it's almost shocking how well the two seem to get along with each other. This, combined with his personal power and continued popularity in the mortal world, are why Athena tolerates him.</li> <li><b>Amphitrite</b>, Goddess of the Seas, who helped overthrow and imprison her husband Poseidon in order to claim her long denied birthright as the daughter of Oceanus.</li> <li><b>Hecate</b>, Goddess of Crossroads and Magic, Protection and Prosperity, who serves Olympus for reasons known only to herself, but also out of friendship with Persephone, Artemis, Hermes and Hestia.</li> </ul>I'm sort of torn on what to do with Herakles and Hebe, as they could work as part of the above group, but I also sort of see them as being loyalists to Zeus. <br /> <br /> Anyway, I just thought it was an interesting idea. Perhaps as part of a setting in which the Aesir have already been through Ragnarok, with Baldr as their leader, and Modi, Magni, Thrud, Vitar, and Hodr as the main gods. And maybe a version of the Aztecs fused with Mexican-Spanish Catholicism.<br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":8}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-22T11:21:13-07:00","id":1082318,"title":"","content":"This is definitely the seed of a really interesting alternate setting for Scion. I'm interested to see how this develops.<br /> <br /> As for Herakles, I can see him being more than happy to help imprison Hera, but maybe having second thoughts about his role afterwards. A potential plot line would be maybe convincing Herakles that Athena was in the wrong and getting him to side against Olympus.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9296-zeldafan42","name":"Zeldafan42","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar9296_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"43"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-22T11:29:56-07:00","id":1082321,"title":"","content":"Herakles in real world Greek religion was often one of the Twelve, he'd make a good role model for your Athena's world. The twelve labors, because they involved bringing the uncivilized under the control of civilization, would link Herakles to empire and exploration.<br /> <br /> Get another male that fits in your pantheon and Athena has her pantheon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-22T14:05:35-07:00","id":1082385,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082321#post1082321\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Herakles in real world Greek religion was often one of the Twelve, he'd make a good role model for your Athena's world. The twelve labors, because they involved bringing the uncivilized under the control of civilization, would link Herakles to empire and exploration.</div> </div> </div> </div>That was my thinking, maybe with Hebe having some sort of unresolved mom issues - &quot;I can be more than a serving wench, Mother! - that motivate her to join in imprisoning her parents.<ul><li><b>Herakles</b>, God of Strength and Heroes, Champion of Olympus, slayer of monsters and conquerer of empires. While big, muscular and hot tempered, he's far more clever and cunning that others give him credit for. At some point, he may even decided to challenge Athena for her throne.</li> <li><b>Hebe</b>, Goddess of Youth and Immortality, and as such claims such things as beauty, passion, idealism and pop culture as her purviews as well. In spite of her fixation on every hip new cultural movement, she still harbors a love of classical western art, mostly because of all the paintings and sculptures they used to make of her.</li> </ul>The pantheon's Virtues would still be Expression, Intellect, Valor and Vengeance. The purview is still Arete, though it's possible they may have figured out aspects of Civitas or Industry under Athena's rulership.<br /> <br /> As for gender parallelism, I figured they went however long with 7 guys and 5 girls, now they can 7 women and 5 men.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":12890,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","user":"No One of Consequence","date":". 04-22-2017, 05:15 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T00:39:58-07:00","id":1082533,"title":"","content":"Seems like a really fun alternative history! A few questions.<br /> <br /> The prophecy is not about Athena. How are you getting around this?<br /> Why are Hades and Poseidon in Tartarus?<br /> Who is ruling Poseidon's kingdom? Amphitrite?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","name":"TwitchTheRat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2447_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T02:00:30-07:00","id":1082541,"title":"","content":"Interesting to see Amphitrite as an Oceanid. She's usually cast as a Nereid (daughter of Nereus and Doris) since she's associated with saltwater and Oceanides are usually freshwater (which is weird to us, but the Ancient Greeks believed the river Okeanus that encircled the world was fresh, and salt water was distinct to the &quot;sea&quot; [i.e. the Mediterranean])","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T02:02:20-07:00","id":1082542,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082533#post1082533\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The prophecy is not about Athena. How are you getting around this?</div> </div> </div> </div> Or is it? As far as I know it's exact wording wasn't part of the story. Could be the &quot;son&quot; element was a sexist assumption.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Check out my expansion to the <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/exalted/185084-autochthonia-house-material\" target=\"_blank\">Realm of Brass and Shadow</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1098-glamourweaver","name":"glamourweaver","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1098_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"7541"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T08:02:54-07:00","id":1082614,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082542#post1082542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or is it? As far as I know it's exact wording wasn't part of the story. Could be the &quot;son&quot; element was a sexist assumption.</div> </div> </div> </div>Or in the modern world Athena could start to identify as male. Not really out of left field.<br /> <br /> <br /> I think Hades would probably side against his siblings though, I don''t think he was terribly fond of either his brothers (as I recall at least), and he does have a reason to hold a grudge against Demeter.<br /> <br /> Anyway I like this, and now I might shamelessly steal this for a sub-plot with Athena getting fed up with Zeus being a freaking jerk.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T08:03:18-07:00","id":1082615,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>glamourweaver</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082542#post1082542\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Or is it? As far as I know it's exact wording wasn't part of the story. Could be the &quot;son&quot; element was a sexist assumption.</div> </div> </div> </div>There are a few different prophecies regarding The Son of Zeus, but the main focus is that it is a son specifically. Since No One Of Consequence's neat little write up here seems to be going with the Metis side of things, then the exact lines are,<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Hesiod: The Theogony</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">For from Metis it was destined that clever children should be born: first a pale-eyed daughter Tritogeneia [Athena], with courage and sound counsel equal to her father's, and <b>then a son</b> she was to bear, <b>king</b> of gods and men, one proud of heart. [Emphasis mine]</div> </div> </div> </div>So, the prophecy regarding the Son of Zeus does specifically mention it being a son, and also actually mentions that it is not Athena. But, there are totally ways around this that we have discussed on various threads here a whole ton.<br /> <br /> Just on some more discussion of the setting though, I <i>can</i> see Poseidon and Hades both getting roped into supporting Zeus during the war due to the theoretical equality of authority they have, though Zeus tends to forget this and place himself as the king of all. I would say that, according to the pattern of these stories, Athena probably had support from the Titans and broke them free of the prison.<br /> <br /> When Zeus rebelled, he broke the Hundred Handed Ones, and the Primordial Cyclopses, and the Giganties from the pit to support him against Cronus, as Cronus had imprisoned them just as Ouranos had done in the past. Following this system, Athena probably jailbroke the Titans, sealed away the original turncoat Titans, and imprisoned the Hundred Handed Ones, the Cyclopses, and the elder Theoi. In doing this, Gaia will speak a prophecy against her that her son shall overthrow her, and be a greater King than she. Gaia in general doesn't want any of her kiddos imprisoned, and that thus far has been the bane of the Greek Deities, the inability to forgive each other, mostly because if they didn't imprison one and other, they would constantly be trying to counter-revolt.<br /> <br /> So, Cronus is probably hanging around as an advisor to Athena, same with the other big Titans. Prometheus too most likely. I can see Hecate having turned coat again, since her primary interest seems to be getting on the winning side.<br /> <br /> Edit: Oh! Oh! Who is replacing Atlas holding up the sky?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Scion 2e Homebrew Projects:<br /> <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1BUnAIZEDk4Zs_W0ELfffeFRAiSel3nAl/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The \u0160iune\u0161</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1MMw0vBmHdvuGUbVhyFw7BpXT49UgECnR/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Enduri</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1CZuVp5VuftwuwHtzrWADqIaq7Vl-LMIr/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Sg\u0101\u2019na Qeda\u2019s</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/17piJ8ZBzvt4PL47vz5Gw3BF6HOZelrfh/view?usp=sharing\" target=\"_blank\">The Abosom</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/282712/Lebor-Oe-In-Dea?src=newest_community\" target=\"_blank\">Lebor \u00d3e In Dea</a>, <a href=\"https://drive.google.com/file/d/1rk9BKLqbUEroFDvuPSQOwp-pBRxSvN9P/view\" target=\"_blank\">The Zemi</a>, <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/324026/Nemetondevos-Revised\" target=\"_blank\">Nemetondevos: Revised</a>, and <a href=\"https://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/325461/Mysteries-of-the-Otherworld\" target=\"_blank\">Mysteries of the Otherworld</a>.","edited":{"userid":394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","user":"Watcher","date":". 04-23-2017, 11:09 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":5}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/394-watcher","name":"Watcher","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"662"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T13:03:12-07:00","id":1082745,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082385#post1082385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> That was my thinking, maybe with Hebe having some sort of unresolved mom issues - &quot;I can be more than a serving wench, Mother! - that motivate her to join in imprisoning her parents.<ul><li><b>Herakles</b>, God of Strength and Heroes, Champion of Olympus, slayer of monsters and conquerer of empires. While big, muscular and hot tempered, he's far more clever and cunning that others give him credit for. At some point, he may even decided to challenge Athena for her throne.</li> <li><b>Hebe</b>, Goddess of Youth and Immortality, and as such claims such things as beauty, passion, idealism and pop culture as her purviews as well. In spite of her fixation on every hip new cultural movement, she still harbors a love of classical western art, mostly because of all the paintings and sculptures they used to make of her.</li> </ul>The pantheon's Virtues would still be Expression, Intellect, Valor and Vengeance. The purview is still Arete, though it's possible they may have figured out aspects of Civitas or Industry under Athena's rulership.<br /> <br /> As for gender parallelism, I figured they went however long with 7 guys and 5 girls, now they can 7 women and 5 men.</div> </div> </div> </div>By the way. Athena and Apollo are normally positive figures, Athena even more than Apollo. Here you've made them villains and seem to be going with a crudely Occidentalist view of the West. Is this a &quot;Kill Whitey!&quot; setting?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T13:20:01-07:00","id":1082752,"title":"","content":"I'll note that a large amount of inspiration for this comes from reading George O'Connor's incredibly amazing series of graphic novels, <a href=\"http://olympiansrule.com\" target=\"_blank\">The Olympians</a>. They are, quite simply, works of beauty and I can not recommend them enthusiastically enough for anyone who has an interest in the Greek pantheon. He's done nine out of the twelve book series, with the tenth (Hermes) coming at the start of next year. The book about Hera continues to be one of my favorites, managing to show her as a power in her own right and much to my enjoyment, presenting the story of Herakles without making her a villain. He also does a cool take on the story of Hades and Persephone, making her something other than a damsel in distress and making Demeter come off as a total badass in one scene. I'm actually sad that he's only doing the twelve (Zeus, Athena, Hera/Herakles, Hades/Persephone/Demeter, Poseidon, Aphrodite, Ares/The Trojan War, Apollo, Artemis, Hermes, Hephaestus and Dionysus), as I'd love to see his takes on the likes of Hecate (who plays a sizable role in Hades), Hestia, and Pan, among others. <br /> <br /> <i>&quot;My children have always been monsters.&quot;</i><div style=\"margin-left:40px\">Poseidon, speaking first about Polyphemus, the cyclops from The Odyssey, and later about Theseus, one of mythology's greatest ass-hats. </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T14:59:28-07:00","id":1082779,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082745#post1082745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> By the way. Athena and Apollo are normally positive figures, Athena even more than Apollo. Here you've made them villains and seem to be going with a crudely Occidentalist view of the West. Is this a &quot;Kill Whitey!&quot; setting?</div> </div> </div> </div>I don't see them as villains here, morally grey sure, and listing some of the nastier names in leadership and philosophy, but then this is pantheon where competing against them and losing gets you turned into a spider or skinned alive, to use examples those very gods performed. Greek gods are terribly flawed individuals, and if, hypothetically, the leaders of Olympus are fated to slide into their worst selves after getting that power and authority it lends an interesting twist to things.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-23T16:08:56-07:00","id":1082811,"title":"","content":"Wait, why would Hades be imprisoned!?<br /> <br /> He was the most well behaved out of all of them! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/eek.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"EEK!\" smilieid=\"9\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T00:40:52-07:00","id":1082930,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Astromancer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082745#post1082745\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> By the way. Athena and Apollo are normally positive figures, Athena even more than Apollo. Here you've made them villains and seem to be going with a crudely Occidentalist view of the West. Is this a &quot;Kill Whitey!&quot; setting?</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200b<span style=\"color:#FF0000\">Kill Whitey? Really? Do I even have to point out how inflammatory that is? Don't do that again.</span>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/OnyxGraphics/OnyxMod.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" /> <span style=\"font-family:georgia\"><span style=\"color:#B22222\"><b>Onyx Path Forum Administrator</b></span></span><br /> <span style=\"color:#FF0000\"><b><span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\">Posts in this color are moderator posts</span></b></span><br /> <span style=\"font-family:comic sans ms\"><span style=\"color:#00FF00\">Posts in this color mean a Great Old One has driven me mad.</span></span><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/onyx-path-announcements/206-forum-rules\" target=\"_blank\"><b>Forum Terms of Use</b></a><br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/contact-us\" target=\"_blank\">the Contact Us link</a>.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/11-darksider","name":"Darksider","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar11_2.jpeg","title":"Forum Administrator","rank":6,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1692"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T02:38:49-07:00","id":1082954,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>No One of Consequence</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082385#post1082385\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> As for gender parallelism, I figured they went however long with 7 guys and 5 girls, now they can 7 women and 5 men.</div> </div> </div> </div>So, basically, you Rule 63'd the pantheon by replacing them with opposite gender counterparts? Gotta say, I don't understand the reason for doing so, other than some ill considered attempt at casting off assigned gender roles. By ill considered, I mean that you removed / replaced some characters but you don't seem to have a good reason as to why you would do so. Hades and Persephone were happy together, it was Persephone's mother who kept trying to break them up. So I find it very odd that she would turn against him, or leave him imprisoned in Greek Hell without some defining reason beyond Athena saying so. Anybody who does actual research into Greek mythology knows that Hades was the most respectable and well behaved member of the entire pantheon, which is why I'm so angry with people today who portray him as some kind of Satanic figure who greedily seeks to claim more souls for the underworld. Its the fucking underworld, everybody ends up there eventually!<br /> <br /> <br /> And Athena isn't supposed to be a goddess of war, she's a <i>goddess of strategy</i> and wisdom. You say in your rewrite that she is always right and yet it sounds like she's behaving exactly like Zeus. The part about her committing brutal atrocities in the name of the greater good makes her come across as a 'do as I say, not as I do' sort of leader, which is extremely detrimental to a goddess who embodies knowledge by turning her subjects into unthinking sheep. At best, she comes across as a ruthless dictator, and we all know how well that turns out in real life, now don't we? Sooner or later, she's going to push somebody too far and then 'wham' she gets assassinated and somebody else takes the throne. Athena shouldn't be trying to continue the cycle of usurpation, she should be trying to break it; preferably by using her vaunted wisdom to create a more enlightened form of governance.<br /> <br /> <br /> And just to point out, you made a typo with Hephaestus. He's married to Aphrodite, not Athena. Not only that, but Aphrodite was having an affair with Ares. If he gets kicked out of Olympus, then it stands to reason that Aphrodite would share a similar fate. That, or go running back to her husband like the fickle mistress she's portrayed as.<br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082779#post1082779\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> I don't see them as villains here, morally grey sure, and listing some of the nastier names in leadership and philosophy, but then this is pantheon where competing against them and losing gets you turned into a spider or skinned alive, to use examples those very gods performed. Greek gods are terribly flawed individuals, and if, hypothetically, the leaders of Olympus are fated to slide into their worst selves after getting that power and authority it lends an interesting twist to things.</div> </div> </div> </div>I believe you're mistaken. It was competing against the gods <b>and winning</b> that got you turned into spiders and stuff. One judge, just one judge said they liked Pan's flute better than Apollo's lute, and that was enough to send Apollo into a psychotic episode. The Greek gods were unbelievable narcissists who became enraged by the thought of public humiliation. For a mortal to challenge them to their own game and come out the victor was unacceptable.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 04-24-2017, 06:11 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T03:49:45-07:00","id":1082957,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082954#post1082954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> <br /> I believe you're mistaken. It was competing against the gods <b>and winning</b> that got you turned into spiders and stuff. One judge, just one judge said they liked Pan's flute better than Apollo's lute, and that was enough to send Apollo into a psychotic episode. The Greek gods were unbelievable narcissists who became enraged by the thought of public humiliation. For a mortal to challenge them to their own game and come out the victor was unacceptable.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, the versions I'm familiar with the mortals invariably lost and it was the idea that they'd even attempt it that enraged the narcissists into petty murder/torture/transformation. The offense was in thinking they could even compete.And it wasn't Pan, it was a different, non godly Saytr named Marysas that was involved in the whole music contest and skinning incident.<br /> <br /> <br /> I agree on your Hades/Persephone views though.<br /> <br /> And I don't think there was anything said in the post about Hephastus being married to Athena, just that there were unrequited feelings. Not sure if that fits the mythology as a whole but I think there was on story with him reasoning that as the two smartest among the gods that they should be involved so it's not out of no where.<br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8375,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","user":"vonpenguin","date":". 04-24-2017, 06:52 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T03:58:59-07:00","id":1082958,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082957#post1082957\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Well, the versions I'm familiar with the mortals invariably lost and it was the idea that they'd even attempt it that enraged the narcissists into petty murder/torture/transformation. The offense was in thinking they could even compete.And it wasn't Pan, it was a different, non godly Saytr named Marysas that was involved in the whole music contest and skinning incident.<br /> <br /></div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Ah, well the 1st edition book describes Pan being the one who got skinned alive, and when Olympus laughed it off as a joke, he got pissed, quit the pantheon and became Satan.<br /> <br /> <br /> But the story of Arachne is basically like this. Arachne said she was the greatest weaver / spinstress in the world. Effectively calling out Athena, who has some influence over this. They challenged each other to a contest of skill and Arache's tapestry ended up being more impressive than Athena's. Enraged by having been shown up in a contest of skill (in front of a crowded audience, no less), she transformed Arachne into a spider. I think there are some versions of the tale in which Arachne is transformed into a monstrous spider-centaur lady and used to guard the Tapestry of Fate","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T04:02:57-07:00","id":1082959,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082958#post1082958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Ah, well the 1st edition book describes Pan being the one who got skinned alive, and when Olympus laughed it off as a joke, he got pissed, quit the pantheon and became Satan.<br /> <br /> <br /> But the story of Arachne is basically like this. Arachne said she was the greatest weaver / spinstress in the world. Effectively calling out Athena, who has some influence over this. They challenged each other to a contest of skill and Arache's tapestry ended up being more impressive than Athena's. Enraged by having been shown up in a contest of skill (in front of a crowded audience, no less), she transformed Arachne into a spider. I think there are some versions of the tale in which Arachne is transformed into a monstrous spider-centaur lady and used to guard the Tapestry of Fate</div> </div> </div> </div>1st edition was also notoriously loose with lore.<br /> <br /> Fair enough, I'm just saying the versions of Arachne's story I've read have all featured her losing, with the details being more horrific the closer the contest.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T05:43:45-07:00","id":1082980,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082959#post1082959\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Fair enough, I'm just saying the versions of Arachne's story I've read have all featured her losing, with the details being more horrific the closer the contest.</div> </div> </div> </div>Well, there are many different versions of various lore. I personally like the versions in which the mortals manage to defeat the Gods in some capacity because it breaks away from the long held stereotype that Gods were invincible entities beyond the reach of mortals. It also helps to reinforce the Greek philosophy of striving to be the best you can be, since under achievers had a shitty afterlife, even if they were good people. Only the best and the brightest got to live in paradise, according to the Greeks. Which is incredibly depressing, even if it did motivate them to greatness. <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/frown.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Frown\" smilieid=\"11\" class=\"inlineimg\" />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T07:19:28-07:00","id":1083001,"title":"","content":"One important fact that often gets left out of the Arachne story: Arachne wasn't really punished for weaving a better tapestry than Athena. She was punished because the tapestry she wove portrayed the Gods in somewhat unflattering ways. She was punished for mocking the gods, not for being a better weaver than Athena.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T10:38:31-07:00","id":1083081,"title":"","content":"Also, in some versions of the Arachne myth, the spider transformation is done out of pity when Arachne attempts to hang herself.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T11:23:14-07:00","id":1083100,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>unnatural1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083001#post1083001\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">One important fact that often gets left out of the Arachne story: Arachne wasn't really punished for weaving a better tapestry than Athena. She was punished because the tapestry she wove portrayed the Gods in somewhat unflattering ways. She was punished for mocking the gods, not for being a better weaver than Athena.</div> </div> </div> </div>There is more than one version.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>SWTOR Referal: <a href=\"http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy\" target=\"_blank\">http://www.swtor.com/r/JQ2nqy</a>","edited":{"userid":414,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","user":"Gallus","date":". 04-24-2017, 02:29 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/414-gallus","name":"Gallus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar414_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"886"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T12:48:06-07:00","id":1083143,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082958#post1082958\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> Ah, well the 1st edition book describes Pan being the one who got skinned alive, and when Olympus laughed it off as a joke, he got pissed, quit the pantheon and became Satan.<br /></div> </div> </div> </div>That was the thing I hated most about 1st edition. It was interesting to be sure, but I would have preferred it if they'd done it with Marsyas instead.<br /> <br /> As for versions of myths, I've always preferred the ones that depict the gods in a positive light, rather than the ones that make them inferior to humanity.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><b>Jason Ross Inczauskis</b>, Freelance Writer<br /> Projects: <i>Dark Eras 2</i>, <i>Mummy: The Curse 2e, Book of Lasting Death, DtR The Clades Companion, Pirates of Pugmire,</i> <i>They Came From Beyond the Grave!, </i><i>TC Aeon: Mission Statements</i>, <i>TC In Media Res,</i> <i>DtD Night Horrors: Enemy Action,</i> <i>C20 Anthology of Dreams</i><br /> Masculine pronouns preferred.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/309-white-oak-dragon","name":"White Oak Dragon","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar309_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"941"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-24T20:39:19-07:00","id":1083304,"title":"","content":"Actually, I like the OP's idea. It's always fun to turn expectations and stereotypes on their ear, and play against type. Assassin Artemis? Tyrant Athena? Why not? The world is ever-changing, the threats are never-ending, and a harder universe leads to harder deities.The only guideline is: does it work for your game idea? If so, then the myths and stories are only guidelines, not hard rules, and shouldn't stop you having fun with whatever you dream up.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5627-purple-snit","name":"Purple Snit","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5627_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"309"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T08:43:46-07:00","id":1083478,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082954#post1082954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Anybody who does actual research into Greek mythology knows that Hades was the most respectable and well behaved member of the entire pantheon,</div> </div> </div> </div>Really? Mr. &quot;I decided the kidnap and rape of my niece was an appropriate thing to do&quot; is more respectable and well behaved than Hebe? Or Iris? I'm not aware of anywhere where Hades and Persephone are described as happy together, though I'd be perfectly willing to be proven wrong on that point.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12989-center-of-all","name":"Center-of-All","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"56"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T14:37:41-07:00","id":1083696,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1082954#post1082954\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">So, basically, you Rule 63'd the pantheon by replacing them with opposite gender counterparts? Gotta say, I don't understand the reason for doing so, other than some ill considered attempt at casting off assigned gender roles. By ill considered, I mean that you removed / replaced some characters but you don't seem to have a good reason as to why you would do so.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sometimes, &quot;because I want to&quot; is all the good reason a creator needs. The slight female dominance of the pantheon is just an organic development of:<ol class=\"decimal\"><li>Athena is the new leader,</li> <li>I don't want any of the first generation gods (the children of Kronos and Rhea) around,</li> <li>Except for Hestia, because she's my all time favorite,</li> <li>I'm doing a take on Athena as a somewhat cold-blooded intellectual type, similar to the Woodrow Wilson style progressive statists of the 20th century</li> <li>Because of that, I want Apollo because of his use by Nietzsche as a symbol of rationalism and order,</li> <li>Artemis goes with her brother, and I think she's cool,</li> <li>Persephone was the first pick to replace Hades, her portrayal being influenced by both the previously mentioned Olympians and Suzanne Santo's &quot;Persephone Under the Earth&quot;</li> <li>Hephaestus is totally besotted with Athena, and I've always liked him,</li> <li>Hermes has a lot of potential in the modern world,</li> <li>I like Hecate,</li> <li>Dionysius because I wanted a devil's advocate, and again because of Nietzsche,</li> <li>There aren't a lot of really notable Greek sea gods, and I decided the version of Amphitrite that had her as daughter of Oceanus seemed to have the most character potential,</li> <li>I was on the fence about Hercules until Astromancer's comment about his connection to the concept of empires,</li> <li>I wanted to keep him with Hebe, who I think is slightly underrated potential wise, and whom I really liked in Marvel's The Incredible Hercules series.</li> </ol>\u200bIt doesn't matter to me one way or the other, though I am aware that there are readers/players/fans who may prefer more airtime for goddesses. It could've ended up as 10 males and 2 women or all women or whatever if that's the way the process had worked out. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> Hades and Persephone were happy together, it was Persephone's mother who kept trying to break them up. So I find it very odd that she would turn against him, or leave him imprisoned in Greek Hell without some defining reason beyond Athena saying so. Anybody who does actual research into Greek mythology knows that Hades was the most respectable and well behaved member of the entire pantheon, which is why I'm so angry with people today who portray him as some kind of Satanic figure who greedily seeks to claim more souls for the underworld. Its the fucking underworld, everybody ends up there eventually! </div> </div> </div>I don't recall saying one way or another about Hades's personality. I actually like him, especially the way he's presented in The Olympians. But since I, a) wanted to remove all the original generation save Hestia, and b) am drawing on certain modern interpretations of the characters, I'm going with a Persephone who married Hades because she wasn't her mother's little girl any more, and has at some point decided that she's not just a consort or wife anymore, but a fully fledged Queen in her own right who doesn't need a king to give her legitimacy. This is not to say that she doesn't have regrets about her choices. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And Athena isn't supposed to be a goddess of war, she's a <i>goddess of strategy</i> and wisdom. You say in your rewrite that she is always right and yet it sounds like she's behaving exactly like Zeus. The part about her committing brutal atrocities in the name of the greater good makes her come across as a 'do as I say, not as I do' sort of leader, which is extremely detrimental to a goddess who embodies knowledge by turning her subjects into unthinking sheep. At best, she comes across as a ruthless dictator, and we all know how well that turns out in real life, now don't we? Sooner or later, she's going to push somebody too far and then 'wham' she gets assassinated and somebody else takes the throne. Athena shouldn't be trying to continue the cycle of usurpation, she should be trying to break it; preferably by using her vaunted wisdom to create a more enlightened form of governance. </div> </div> </div>I'm presenting her as a modern intellectual progressive, the same sort of people who think totalitarian rule by the intellectual elites (i.e., technocracy) is a perfectly &quot;enlightened&quot; form of government. The same sort of people who created the European Union and, with only two real exceptions, have held the office of US President for the entire 20th century. Part of this derives from Plato's musings about Athena as mind and intellect, as well as Neal Stephenson's idea that she's meant to be the goddess of technological innovation. I like Athena quite a bit, but I wanted to do a riff on 20th century progressive technocracy, and being the smartest person in the room doesn't always mean being the most humane. Or the most wise, as the story of the Judgment of Paris shows. <br /> <br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> And just to point out, you made a typo with Hephaestus. He's married to Aphrodite, not Athena. Not only that, but Aphrodite was having an affair with Ares. If he gets kicked out of Olympus, then it stands to reason that Aphrodite would share a similar fate. That, or go running back to her husband like the fickle mistress she's portrayed as. </div> </div> </div>That's not a mistake. He's frequently presented as being besotted with Athena, as she's the only one who really understands his technological genius. This is to the point when he once tried to force himself upon her, only to have his seed spill on the ground, giving birth to Erichithonious, future king of Athens. Aphrodite's marriage to the ugly, crippled nerd of the forge is often presented as not being her idea, and she's much more attracted to the super-macho stud of a war god that is he brother in law. Likely as some sort of commentary on the natural connection between sex and violence, or something. <br /> <br /> (And the other reason for Ares's absence is that it's first iteration was as part of a Mutants &amp; Masterminds setting in which Ares was this wandering anti-hero in the vein of Casca the Eternal Mercenary, going about fighting people with his big red sword. But he and Athena just don't get along, so any pantheon that has her in change just isn't going to have him around. At least IMO.)<br /> <br /> <br /> <br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><i>What is tolerance? It is the consequence of humanity. We are all formed of frailty and error; let us pardon reciprocally each other's folly. That is the first law of nature.</i><br /> Voltaire, &quot;Tolerance&quot; (1764)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12890-no-one-of-consequence","name":"No One of Consequence","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12890_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3937"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T14:47:08-07:00","id":1083701,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Center-of-All</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083478#post1083478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Really? Mr. &quot;I decided the kidnap and rape of my niece was an appropriate thing to do&quot; is more respectable and well behaved than Hebe? Or Iris? I'm not aware of anywhere where Hades and Persephone are described as happy together, though I'd be perfectly willing to be proven wrong on that point.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Sure, everybody knows about Hades seeking love under less than ideal circumstances. But aside from that, what villainous acts has he truly committed? You'll be hard pressed to find any, I'm sure. And if you do, it's nothing compared to the laundry list of atrocities the others were responsible for. Heck, he even assisted the heroes in the past by lending them his magic helmet, <i>which makes the wearer invisible!</i>","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 04-25-2017, 05:51 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T15:01:26-07:00","id":1083707,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Center-of-All</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083478#post1083478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Really? Mr. &quot;I decided the kidnap and rape of my niece was an appropriate thing to do&quot; is more respectable and well behaved than Hebe? Or Iris? I'm not aware of anywhere where Hades and Persephone are described as happy together, though I'd be perfectly willing to be proven wrong on that point.</div> </div> </div> </div>Honestly about 80%+ of the interpretations I've read have viewed it as more of an elopement situation. One of them even implies that Hades courted Persephone while making an arranged marriage deal and Demeter was mostly upset because them eloping meant Hades skipped out on paying a dowry.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T16:07:48-07:00","id":1083738,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Center-of-All</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083478#post1083478\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Really? Mr. &quot;I decided the kidnap and rape of my niece was an appropriate thing to do&quot; is more respectable and well behaved than Hebe? Or Iris? I'm not aware of anywhere where Hades and Persephone are described as happy together, though I'd be perfectly willing to be proven wrong on that point.</div> </div> </div> </div>It's all relative. We can look back upon it now and understand it to be a bad thing, but it was somewhat standard behavior back then. It's like calling someone in the past &quot;Racist&quot;. Well, yes. Most people were. It's not a good thing. But compared to what Poseidon and Zeus did, it was pretty damn civil and tame.<br /> <br /> Especially if you add in the fact that there may not have been any rape involved.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2447-twitchtherat","name":"TwitchTheRat","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2447_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"134"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T17:34:10-07:00","id":1083761,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>TwitchTheRat</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083738#post1083738\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It's all relative. We can look back upon it now and understand it to be a bad thing, but it was somewhat standard behavior back then. It's like calling someone in the past &quot;Racist&quot;. Well, yes. Most people were. It's not a good thing. But compared to what Poseidon and Zeus did, it was pretty damn civil and tame.<br /> <br /> Especially if you add in the fact that there may not have been any rape involved.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> Very true. One has to remember that society had very different views at the time. Otherwise, if we judge people of history by the norms of today, then every past civilization would be a shit hole by today's standards.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-25T23:46:38-07:00","id":1083874,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083761#post1083761\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Very true. One has to remember that society had very different views at the time. Otherwise, if we judge people of history by the norms of today, then every past civilization would be a shit hole by today's standards.</div> </div> </div> </div>The civilizations of the past were definitely shit holes by today's standards, I don't think there's much contesting that. But on the subject of judging their occupants for that, I'm reminded of Dante's take on salvation, in which the entirety of the human population to be born and die prior to the advent of Christianity are doomed to spend eternity in hell for the simple crime of hitting the bucket before John got around to inventing baptism. Granted it was the least nasty circle of hell, but still.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13075-malicwanderer","name":"MalicWanderer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T04:14:23-07:00","id":1083909,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>MalicWanderer</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083874#post1083874\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">The civilizations of the past were definitely shit holes by today's standards, I don't think there's much contesting that. But on the subject of judging their occupants for that, I'm reminded of Dante's take on salvation, in which the entirety of the human population to be born and die prior to the advent of Christianity are doomed to spend eternity in hell for the simple crime of hitting the bucket before John got around to inventing baptism. Granted it was the least nasty circle of hell, but still.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> <br /> Actually, the story of Jesus says that he spent the 3 days he was dead wandering around the Underworld and saving the condemned souls who died before him. I think there's even a story of him strolling right into Hades and stealing people from... well, Hades.<br /> <br /> And prior to Christianity there was no belief in Hell, or at least not in the capacity we know it today. Jews don't believe in that stuff, so when you die, you're just dead. Waiting for the messiah to come, in which the dead shall rise from their graves and go to join God. Which is why they don't cremate their dead. Can't come back to life if you're just ash and bones.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1394,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","user":"Nyrufa","date":". 04-26-2017, 07:16 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T04:39:36-07:00","id":1083914,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083909#post1083909\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> <br /> <br /> Actually, the story of Jesus says that he spent the 3 days he was dead wandering around the Underworld and saving the condemned souls who died before him. I think there's even a story of him strolling right into Hades and stealing people from... well, Hades.<br /> <br /> And prior to Christianity there was no belief in Hell, or at least not in the capacity we know it today. Jews don't believe in that stuff, so when you die, you're just dead. Waiting for the messiah to come, in which the dead shall rise from their graves and go to join God. Which is why they don't cremate their dead. Can't come back to life if you're just ash and bones.</div> </div> </div> </div>Huh. Then I wonder why Jews also don't believe in embalming their dead? You'd think they'd be kind of obsessive about keeping the body as intact as possible?","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T04:45:36-07:00","id":1083915,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>unnatural1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083914#post1083914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Huh. Then I wonder why Jews also don't believe in embalming their dead? You'd think they'd be kind of obsessive about keeping the body as intact as possible?</div> </div> </div> </div>Embalming does tend to involve taking out the more easily rotted organs and replacing fluids with preservatives doesn't it?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8375-vonpenguin","name":"vonpenguin","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"246"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T04:51:18-07:00","id":1083917,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>vonpenguin</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083915#post1083915\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Embalming does tend to involve taking out the more easily rotted organs and replacing fluids with preservatives doesn't it?</div> </div> </div> </div>Oops. Yeah, that's probably why.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T05:35:06-07:00","id":1083927,"title":"","content":"Also, should keep in mind that Dante wasn't exactly a priest. He was a poet and his description of Hell (multiple layers with their own forms of punishment) wasn't described in the actual mythology. Hell was described as being the 'second death' in which the souls sent to burn there would become ashes beneath the feet of the faithful after the time of salvation.The Biblical Hell isn't a place of eternal torment, it's a crematorium for lost souls. Only those who gained entry to Heaven were / are granted eternal life.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1394-nyrufa","name":"Nyrufa","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1394_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6374"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T06:20:28-07:00","id":1083936,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Nyrufa</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083927#post1083927\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Also, should keep in mind that Dante wasn't exactly a priest. He was a poet and his description of Hell (multiple layers with their own forms of punishment) wasn't described in the actual mythology. Hell was described as being the 'second death' in which the souls sent to burn there would become ashes beneath the feet of the faithful after the time of salvation.The Biblical Hell isn't a place of eternal torment, it's a crematorium for lost souls. Only those who gained entry to Heaven were / are granted eternal life.</div> </div> </div> </div>\u200bThe Inferno was also basically Dante's self-insert fanfic in which he was the star character and got to hang out with his favorite historical figures. It has next to no actual mythological value.<br /> <br /> \u200bBut don't take my word for it! This lady does a much better job of breaking down Dante's works than I ever could! Enjoy! <img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/smilies/smile.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" title=\"Smile\" smilieid=\"1\" class=\"inlineimg\" /><br /> <br /> <div class=\"videocontainer with-aspect-ratio videocontainer-horizontal\" style=\"aspect-ratio: 16/9;\"> <a class=\"video-frame\" href=\"https://youtu.be/gp8JGQk0CFQ\" data-code=\"gp8JGQk0CFQ\" data-provider=\"youtube_share\" data-height=\"0\" data-width=\"0\" itemprop=\"video\"> <div class=\"videothumbnail\" style=\"\"> <img src=\"https://img.youtube.com/vi/gp8JGQk0CFQ/hqdefault.jpg\" alt=\"\" /> <div class=\"videothumbnailoverlay\"></div> </div> </a> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\">&quot;We have done the impossible, and that makes us mighty.&quot;<br /> Captain Malcolm Reynolds</div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/679-unnatural1","name":"unnatural1","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"350"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T13:06:35-07:00","id":1084116,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>unnatural1</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/scion/1082292-an-alternate-olympus-dodekatheon-theoi?p=1083914#post1083914\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\"><br /> Huh. Then I wonder why Jews also don't believe in embalming their dead? You'd think they'd be kind of obsessive about keeping the body as intact as possible?</div> </div> </div> </div>as far as I know, the reason is that Judaism took quit literally the part of &quot;for dust thou art, and unto dust shalt thou return&quot;. You are commanded to be buried in dirt, because you were made out of it. Just as Adam was created out of earth so does anyone who was turned into dust could be revived once more- but if you burn the body, you don't &quot;return to earth&quot;, and as such you can't be reborn.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=Shachar%20Gold\" target=\"_blank\">Check my STV content</a>, <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/28219-my-weekly-wod-project-program\" target=\"_blank\">Or My Homebrew</a><br /> <br /> &quot;And all our knowledge is, Ourselves to know&quot;- An Essay on Man<br /> <br /> I now blog in <a href=\"https://chroniclesoflostlight.wordpress.com/\" target=\"_blank\">here</a>","edited":{"userid":503,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","user":"LostLight","date":". 04-27-2017, 10:42 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/503-lostlight","name":"LostLight","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar503_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4531"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-04-26T15:19:33-07:00","id":1084182,"title":"","content":"I wasn't really putting Inferno forward as a proper description of Christian belief, just comparing the concept of damning people for being born to the wrong time and place. If anything the fact that Dante's not in any way an actual authority on much of anything and his epic is mostly creepy stalker fanfic just reinforces the point that maybe that's not a thing you should do. :P<br /> <br /> As to having no mythological value, I'd agree except for the point that it's somehow become like, the definitive source for details about hell re: popular culture. Almost any piece of fiction I've ever seen that depicts hell draws from Dante's work, at least partially. So while it's about worthless as a look into actual Christian mythos (his hell has more from Greek mythology in it than Christian, as I recall) it's definitely a story with some staying power. That's probably worth at least something, I'd imagine, to a game about stories and mythic resonance.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13075-malicwanderer","name":"MalicWanderer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13075_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}